    <h2>2. Technical Challenges and Refactoring</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>1. Generic C# Type Parsing:</strong> We initially planned to write a regex to parse generic return types, like nested lists and tasks. However, this approach proved prone to syntax errors due to the complexity of C# type signatures. To resolve this, we focused on looking for class modifiers and return keywords at the start of the line combined with a comma-and-space parameter check, which proved highly reliable and prevented false positives.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>2. String Preprocessor Precedence:</strong> During testing, a bug occurred where the preprocessor removed comments before string literals. This caused lines containing double slashes inside strings (such as URLs) to be split incorrectly. We resolved this by changing the cleaning order to mask string literals before stripping comments.
    </p>

    <h2>3. System Capabilities & Honest Assessment</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>System Capabilities:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Unified Diff Line Mapping:</strong> The stateful parser tracks line offsets from hunk headers, ensuring comments land on the correct lines in the pull request.</li>
        <li><strong>Lexical Cleaning:</strong> The preprocessor strips comments and string literals to prevent false positives.</li>
        <li><strong>Deduplication Registry:</strong> The registry checks existing comments using a file-line-category lookup, preventing duplicate notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Automatic Skipping:</strong> The file gate filters out binary assets, deleted files, and auto-generated code, focusing reviews only on modified source files.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <strong>System Limitations:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Multi-Line Declarations:</strong> The scanner processes code line-by-line. If a method signature is split across multiple lines, the parameter count check may fail to detect SRP violations.</li>
        <li><strong>Fork PR Permissions:</strong> Pull requests from external repository forks run with a read-only token. If write operations fail, the Action logs a warning and exits cleanly without crashing the workflow.</li>
    </ul>

</body>
